    "created_at": "2018-02-13T17:48:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-02-13T17:48:23Z",
    "body": "I'd recommend reading a little about the testharness API [here](http://web-platform-tests.org/writing-tests/testharness-api.html), and then modeling an `async_test` off of something like https://github.com/w3c/web-platform-tests/blob/master/html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements/loading-the-media-resource/resource-selection-invoke-insert-source.html#L8.\r\n\r\nYour test should trigger the resource selection algorithm. This can be done by adding a `<source>` child to a media element (line 11 of example). This'll set it's `delay-the-loading-event` flag, and then JavaScript will continue execution (step 4). Remember, when we re-enter the algorithm we eventually want to hit the last condition of step 6. We just added a `<source>` element which will match the third condition of step 6, so in our JS we'll want to quickly remove it so when the algorithm resumes, we hit the very last condition of step 6 (there's no longer a `<source>` child). Here the spec should be amended to *unset* the `delay-the-loading-event` flag. Your test should make sure that the `load` event gets fired since there should be nothing delaying the loading of it.\r\n\r\n### In short:\r\n\r\n  1. Modify something like the example test I included to remove the source element right after adding it\r\n  2. Verify that `window.onload` is indeed called (and not still delayed). Optionally you can verify that it is called *after* `mediaElementHere.onloadstart` too. Hope that helps.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
